ice-<hash>". Since the user tried "user-service" and it didn\'t work, maybe the deployment has a different name. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the deployment is named "user-service" and the replicaset is managed under that. The error might be because the user is using the wrong name. So, the correct approach is to first list all deployments to find the exact name, then use that to get the replicaset. \n\nIn the previous step, the user ran "kubectl get deployments -n social-network" and got a list. Let me check that output again. The deployments listed include "user-service" as a deployment. So the replicaset should be "user-service-<some-hash>". The user tried "user-service" which is incorrect. \n\nTherefore, the next step is to describe the deployment to find the correct replicaset name. Alternatively, use "kubectl get replicaset" with the correct name. But since the user can\'t get the replicaset directly, maybe they should check the deployment\'s status. \n\nAlternatively, check the deployment\'s YAML to see the replicasets. But the user\'s available tools include "kubectl get deployment" and "kubectl describe deployment". So, the next step is to describe the deployment to get more details, including the associated replicaset. \n\nSo, the correct tool call would be to describe the deployment "user-service" to find out the replicaset name and other configuration details that might indicate the issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9a494736e73d8476', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 701, 'prompt_tokens': 4463, 'total_tokens': 5164, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd63-249b-74f2-8c11-ad3afcfd2c60-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9a494736e73d8476', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4463, 'output_tokens': 701, 'total_tokens': 5164}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                   user-service\nNamespace:              social-network\nCreationTimestamp:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 19:59:42 +0000\nLabels:                 app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=Helm\n                        service=user-service\nAnnotations:            deployment.kubernetes.io/revision: 1\n                        meta.helm.sh/release-name: social-network\n                        meta.helm.sh/release-namespace: social-network\nSelector:               service=user-service\nReplicas:               1 desired | 1 updated | 1 total | 0 available | 1 unavailable\nStrategyType:           RollingUpdate\nMinReadySeconds:        0\nRollingUpdateStrategy:  25% max unavailable, 25% max surge\nPod Template:\n  Labels:  app=user-service\n           service=user-service\n  Containers:\n   user-service:\n    Image:      docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\n    Port:       9090/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      UserService\n    Environment:  <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /social-network-microservices/config/jaeger-config.yml from user-service-config (rw,path="jaeger-config.yml")\n      /social-network-microservices/config/service-config.json from user-service-config (rw,path="service-config.json")\n   sidecar:\n    Image:      busybox:latest\n    Port:       9090/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      sh\n      -c\n      exec nc -lk -p 9090\n    Environment:  <none>\n    Mounts:       <none>\n  Volumes:\n   user-service-config:\n    Type:          ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:          user-service\n    Optional:      false\n  Node-Selectors:  <none>\n  Tolerations:     <none>\nConditions:\n  Type           Status  Reason\n  ----           ------  ------\n  Available      False   MinimumReplicasUnavailable\n  Progressing    True    ReplicaSetUpdated\nOldReplicaSets:  <none>\nNewReplicaSet:   user-service-bc5bc5bc4 (1/1 replicas created)\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason             Age   From                   Message\n  ----    ------             ----  ----                   -------\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  86s   deployment-controller  Scaled up replica set user-service-bc5bc5bc4 to 1\n', id='340e8c59-343f-4947-9bda-f7a28722a0f4', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9a494736e73d8476'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d373e446-d90a-4b1f-9bc9-f67cde7e3c6b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service deployment has 1 desired replica, but only 0 are available. The event says "MinimumReplicasUnavailable," which usually means the pods can\'t reach the desired state. The pod is in an Error state with 2 restarts. Earlier logs didn\'t show errors, but maybe there\'s an issue with the container image or resources.\n\nWait, the deployment\'s Pod Template uses the image "docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest". Maybe the image is incorrect or not accessible. Also, the pod\'s status might be failing due to an error in the container. Since the logs didn\'t show errors, perhaps the container is crashing immediately. \n\nI should check the pod\'s events for more details. The "kubectl describe pod" command would show events related to the pod\'s lifecycle, like crashes or startup failures. Also, checking the resource limits in the deployment might help. If the pod is hitting memory or CPU limits, that could cause it to crash. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the ConfigMap referenced in the pod template is missing or misconfigured. The ConfigMap is "user-service" and mounts two files. If those files are missing, the container might fail to start. \n\nAnother angle: the deployment uses a RollingUpdate strategy with 25% max unavailable. But since there\'s only one replica, maybe the rollout is causing issues. However, the event mentions "ReplicaSetUpdated," so the new replica set is created but not available. \n\nI need to check the pod\'s detailed status and events. Let me run "kubectl describe pod user-service-bc5bc5bc4 -n social-network" again to see if there are any new events or errors. Also, checking the pod\'s resource usage could be useful. Maybe the container is running out of memory or CPU. \n\nWait, the user already ran "kubectl describe pod" earlier, but maybe there\'s more info in the events. Alternatively, checking the pod\'s logs again with "kubectl logs" might show errors if the container exited quickly. But the previous logs didn\'t have errors. \n\nAnother possibility: the container image is incorrect. Maybe the image name is wrong, or the registry is unreachable. The image is "docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest". Maybe the image isn\'t pulling correctly. \n\nI should also check the deployment\'s resource requests and limits. If the pod is requesting more resources than available, it might be evicted. Using "kubectl describe deployment" shows the pod template, but maybe the resources are not set. \n\nAlternatively, the sidecar container might be causing issues. The sidecar is using "busybox:latest" and running a netcat server. If that\'s not working, maybe it\'s affecting the main container. \n\nHmm, the user-service pod is in an Error state with 2 restarts. The logs didn\'t show errors, but maybe the container is crashing due to an unhandled exception or a missing dependency. \n\nI think the next step is to check the pod\'s events and logs again, and also verify the image and resource settings. Let me run "kubectl describe pod" again to see if there are any new events or details I missed.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe `user-service` deployment has 0 available replicas and the pod is in an `Error` state. To investigate further, I need to check the pod\'s detailed events and status for clues about why it\'s failing. The `kubectl describe pod` command will provide information about container crashes, image pull errors, or resource issues.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod user-service-bc5bc5bc4 -n social-network"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 780, 'prompt_tokens': 4193, 'total_tokens': 4973, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd63-48c4-7241-abab-5c2f0405785a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4193, 'output_tokens': 780, 'total_tokens': 4973}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='93c069d1-8e54-41aa-83ba-0a36718676e5'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on with the user-service pod. The user mentioned that the pod is in an Error state with 2 restarts, but the logs don\'t show any explicit errors. The deployment shows 0 available replicas, which means the pod isn\'t running as expected.\n\nFirst, I checked the deployment and saw that the ReplicaSet is user-service-bc5bc5bc4 with 1 pod created. But when I tried to get the ReplicaSet, it wasn\'t found. Maybe the deployment is using a different name or there\'s a typo. Wait, the deployment name is user-service, and the ReplicaSet is named user-service-bc5bc5bc4. That makes sense because Kubernetes appends a random string to the ReplicaSet name based on the deployment\'s name and the revision.\n\nNext, I used kubectl describe pod on the user-service-bc5bc5bc4 pod. The output might have more details about why the pod is failing. The logs didn\'t show errors, so maybe the issue is with the container\'s entry point or the image. Let me check the pod\'s events and container statuses. If the container is crashing, the events might mention reasons like exit codes, image pull errors, or resource limits. Also, checking the pod\'s status for any "Error" messages or "CrashLoopBackOff" could help. If the container is exiting with a non-zero code, that\'s a clue. Maybe the image isn\'t pulling correctly, or there\'s a configuration issue in the ConfigMap. I should also check the resource limits in the deployment to see if the pod is being terminated due to OOM or CPU limits. Alternatively, there might be a problem with the sidecar container or the network configuration since the service uses Thrift RPCs. Let me look into the pod\'s describe output for more clues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod user-service-bc5bc5bc4 -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b3c52906fb3aaf30', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 426, 'prompt_tokens': 5211, 'total_tokens': 5637, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd63-6d52-7cd0-bd52-890ab80a8d1b-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod user-service-bc5bc5bc4 -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b3c52906fb3aaf30', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5211, 'output_tokens': 426, 'total_tokens': 5637}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             user-service-bc5bc5bc4-b6g54\nNamespace:        social-network\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node1.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.32\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 19:59:43 +0000\nLabels:           app=user-service\n                  pod-template-hash=bc5bc5bc4\n                  service=user-service\nAnnotations:      <none>\nStatus:           Running\nIP:               10.244.1.63\nIPs:\n  IP:           10.244.1.63\nControlled By:  ReplicaSet/user-service-bc5bc5bc4\nContainers:\n  user-service:\n    Container ID:  docker://e2fb16c3f0a5d829c073bdc6289e6e190b76679d49beab970991914ee4761416\n    Image:         docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\n    Image ID:      docker-pullable://deathstarbench/social-network-microservices@sha256:a845266e98ce15d204ee7b98d41a2a95820af525013ce70f14104f5b9b5e92b6\n    Port:          9090/TCP\n    Host Port:     0/TCP\n    Command:\n      UserService\n    State:          Running\n      Started:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 19:59:46 +0000\n    Ready:          True\n    Restart Count:  0\n    Environment:    <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /social-network-microservices/config/jaeger-config.yml from user-service-config (rw,path="jaeger-config.yml")\n      /social-network-microservices/config/service-config.json from user-service-config (rw,path="service-config.json")\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-7fjn9 (ro)\n  sidecar:\n    Container ID:  docker://f0a3a61eab02f72549e3142337cb68b220e349e98d555bcfbc8335911cea4c73\n    Image:         busybox:latest\n    Image ID:      docker-pullable://busybox@sha256:b3255e7dfbcd10cb367af0d409747d511aeb66dfac98cf30e97e87e4207dd76f\n    Port:          9090/TCP\n    Host Port:     0/TCP\n    Command:\n      sh\n      -c\n      exec nc -lk -p 9090\n    State:          Waiting\n      Reason:       CrashLoopBackOf