2026-03-17 20:03:21 - DEBUG - all.stratus.rollback - [Loop 2] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='You are a smart and capable tool using agent. You are an expert at remediating the faults in IT environments. You have tons of experience with kubernetes and SRE tools.\nYour responsibility is to rollback the changes that were made to the system to mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident. You are given a rollback tool to help you rollback the system.\nYou are given a system and a rollback tool. The system is either in a initial faulty state, or in the state you left it incompletely repaired after the previous round of mitigation. You need to rollback the system to the initial state since the operations you performed in the previous round may negatively impact your current round of mitigation.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) rollback: You should run the rollback tool as many times as you can, since this rollback tool undos the last operation performed on the system. You should run the rollback tool until it returns that there are no more operations to undo. If you believe you\'ve finished the task, you should submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool to trigger the end of the task.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4602781c-da9f-4e97-8fc8-a97cc02fe07c'), HumanMessage(content="In each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind. When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ac0af242-5744-4503-8830-401c1a584f3d'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: rollback_command\n\ntool description: Use this function to roll back the last kubectl command you observed\nWhen you call this tool multiple times, you will roll-back previous commands in the reverse order of their prior execution."\n\n\ntool name: get_previous_rollbackable_cmd\n\ntool description: Use this function to get a list of commands you previously executed that could be roll-backed.\n\n\ntool name: r_submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit after you rolled back all the changes.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='43612037-42b7-4af4-895b-9accce0f3b7b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is asking me to rollback changes in an IT environment, specifically using Kubernetes and SRE tools. The main goal is to revert the system to its initial state by undoing the last operations. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out which tool to use. The available tools are wait_tool, rollback_command, get_previous_rollbackable_cmd, and r_submit_tool. \n\nThe user mentioned that the rollback tool undoes the last operation, and they might need to run it multiple times. But to do that, I should first check what commands are available to rollback. The get_previous_rollbackable_cmd tool seems useful here because it lists the commands that can be rolled back. Without knowing which commands were executed, I can't effectively use rollback_command. \n\nSo, the first step should be to retrieve the list of previous commands using get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. Once I have that list, I can determine the order and which commands to undo. However, the user's initial instruction says to run the rollback tool as many times as possible until there are no more operations to undo. But if I don't know the commands, maybe I should start by getting the list. \n\nWait, but the rollback_command can be called multiple times to undo previous commands in reverse order. However, if there are no commands to rollback, it might not do anything. So maybe the correct approach is to first check the available commands. But the user's task says to rollback until no more operations are left. So perhaps the initial step is to use get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to find out which commands are available. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the rollback_command can be called directly, but if there are no commands, it would just exit. But the user wants to ensure that all changes are rolled back. So the safest approach is to first get the list of commands that can be rolled back. That way, I can process them in the correct order. \n\nWait, but the user's instruction says to run the rollback tool as many times as possible. So maybe the first step is to call rollback_command repeatedly until it can't anymore. But if there are no commands, it would stop. However, without knowing the commands, maybe the system has some history. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_previous_rollbackable_cmd is necessary to list the commands, then use rollback_command on each. But the user's task says to run the rollback tool (which is rollback_command) as many times as possible. So perhaps the correct first step is to call get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to get the list, then iterate through them. But the user's current step is the thinking stage, and the next step is to choose a tool. \n\nWait, the user is in the thinking stage and needs to choose a tool. The available tools are the four mentioned. The user's task is to rollback changes. So the first action should be to get the list of commands that can be rolled back. Therefore, the correct tool to use here is get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. That way, I can know which commands to undo. \n\nBut maybe the user expects to start rolling back immediately. However, without knowing the commands, it's risky. So the first step is to get the list. Therefore, the tool choice is get_previous_rollbackable_cmd.\n</think>\n\nget_previous_rollbackable_cmd\n\nI need to first identify which commands were previously executed that can be roll-backed. This will allow me to systematically undo the changes in reverse order of their execution. The get_previous_rollbackable_cmd tool provides the necessary list of candidate commands for rollback operations.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 740, 'prompt_tokens': 621, 'total_tokens': 1361, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd64-db7c-7dc0-a101-219e00269065-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 621, 'output_tokens': 740, 'total_tokens': 1361}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='442fc651-1cd1-4bd7-83a2-563bca51af6b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, so the user wants me to generate a tool call based on the previously chosen tool, which was get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. Let me think about this.\n\nFirst, the tool's name is get_previous_rollbackable_cmd, and its description says it's used to get a list of commands that can be roll-backed. Since the user mentioned that the rollback tool undoes the last operation, I need to retrieve the list of commands that were executed and are eligible for rollback. \n\nThe function doesn't require any parameters, so the tool call should be straightforward. I just need to call the function with an empty arguments object. This will return the list of commands, which I can then use to determine the order of rollback. \n\nWait, the user also mentioned that when using rollback_command multiple times, it rolls back previous commands in the reverse order of their execution. So the list from get_previous_rollbackable_cmd should be in the order they were executed, and I need to reverse that to undo them correctly. But first, I need to get the list. \n\nTherefore, the correct tool call here is to invoke get_previous_rollbackable_cmd with no arguments. That's the only step needed now. Once I have that list, the next steps would involve using rollback_command for each entry in the list. But for this round, the task is just to generate the tool call for getting the list.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_previous_rollbackable_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-89e0fb8917427049', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 309, 'prompt_tokens': 1107, 'total_tokens': 1416, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd64-fcbe-7232-aa61-4e4e82b2efca-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_previous_rollbackable_cmd', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-89e0fb8917427049', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1107, 'output_tokens': 309, 'total_tokens': 1416}), ToolMessage(content='', id='f3a5f426-6a5b-4704-9f21-3f59be60dcf0', t